Day 36: Expense Tracker That Saves to a File

📌 Project Overview

This is my Day 36 Python project, where I built an Expense Tracker that saves expenses to a text file so they remain available even after the program closes.

The program allows users to add expenses, view all saved expenses, calculate the total amount spent, and exit the application.

Unlike my earlier projects that stored data only in variables, this project introduces file handling, allowing my program to keep data between runs.

🎯 Features
✅ Add expenses with a description and amount.
✅ Save expenses permanently in expenses.txt.
✅ View all saved expenses with numbered entries.
✅ Display expense descriptions in title case.
✅ Calculate the total amount spent.
✅ Reject negative expense amounts.
✅ Prevent commas in descriptions from breaking the file format by replacing them with spaces.
✅ Handle a missing expense file using try/except FileNotFoundError.
✅ Keep the menu running until the user chooses Exit.
✅ Display a message when no expense file exists.


🧠 Why Is File Handling Important?

Before this project, most of my programs stored information in variables. When the program stopped, that information was lost.

A file works like a notebook on your computer. The program can write information into it and read that information later.

For example, an expense saved as:

Tea, 20.0
Bus ticket, 35.0

remains in expenses.txt after the program closes. When I run the program again, I can read those saved expenses.


⚙️ How the Program Works
1. Adding an Expense

The program asks for a description and amount, then saves the expense in append mode.

description = input("Enter description: ").replace(",", " ")
amount = float(input("Enter amount: "))

if amount < 0:
    print("Amount cannot be negative.")
else:
    with open("expenses.txt", "a") as file:
        file.write(f"{description}, {amount}\n")

How it works:

Read the expense description.
Replace commas in the description with spaces.
Convert the amount to a floating-point number using float().
Reject the amount if it is negative.
Open the file in append mode ("a").
Write the expense to a new line.
Display a confirmation message.

Why use append mode?

Append mode adds new information at the end of the file without deleting existing expenses. If the file does not exist, Python creates it.

2. Viewing All Expenses

The program opens the file in read mode and processes each line.

with open("expenses.txt", "r") as file:
    number = 1

    for line in file:
        description, amount = line.strip().split(",")
        print(f"{number}.{description.title()} -₹{float(amount)}")
        number += 1

How it works:

"r" opens the file for reading.
line.strip() removes surrounding whitespace, including the newline character.
.split(",") separates the description from the amount.
float(amount) converts the stored amount back into a number.
.title() capitalizes the first letter of each word in the displayed description.
number += 1 increases the entry number after each expense.

The program uses try/except FileNotFoundError to display No expenses yet. if the file does not exist.

3. Calculating Total Expenses

The program starts with a total of zero and adds each saved expense amount.

total = 0

with open("expenses.txt", "r") as file:
    for line in file:
        description, amount = line.strip().split(",")
        total += float(amount)

print(f"Total spent: ₹{total}")

For example:

Tea: ₹20
Bus ticket: ₹35

Total:

[
20 + 35 = 55
]

The output is:

Total spent: ₹55.0

The program also handles a missing file by displaying No expenses yet..

4. Exiting the Program

The while True loop repeatedly displays the menu. When the user selects option 4, the program prints Bye and uses break to stop the loop.


📂 Understanding File Modes
Mode	Meaning	Used in This Project?
"r"	Read existing file contents	✅ Yes
"a"	Append new contents without deleting existing data	✅ Yes
"w"	Write from scratch, replacing existing contents	❌ Not used
Why use with open()?

The with statement automatically closes the file when the indented block finishes. This helps manage files safely without manually calling file.close().


